<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tovsta Mohyla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tolstaya Mogila</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tovsta Mohyla is a Scythian royal burial mound of the 4th century BCE near the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intercession-of-the-theotokos">Pokrov</a> in Dnipropetrovsk region, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Excavated by Borys Mozolevsky in 1971, it yielded a gold pectoral decorated with scenes of Scythian daily life and of animals in combat, one of the finest works of Greek goldsmiths made for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>. The pectoral is now in the Museum of Historical Treasures of Ukraine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tovsta-mohyla.jpg" alt="Tovsta Mohyla" width="320" height="122" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_view_of_the_Tovsta_Mohyla_kurhan_(1970).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
